#!/usr/bin/env node
// ci-green: reads a commit's GitHub Actions workflow-run conclusions and says whether CI is green.
//
//   ci-green <sha> --pr <n> [--wait] [--pushed-at <iso>] [--task <id>]
//
// Run it from inside a clone of the repository: it fetches origin, reads `.github/ci-green.json`
// at the SHA (`git show`), diffs the SHA against its merge base with origin/main, and queries
// GitHub through `gh`, which infers the repository from the clone.
//
//   --pr <n>          the pull request number (required); used to fetch its head when the SHA is
//                     not local and to explain a SHA that got no runs.
//   --wait            block up to 540 seconds (CI_GREEN_WAIT_SECONDS) while runs are pending, and
//                     rerun an infrastructure red once. Without it the command reads once.
//   --pushed-at <iso> when the SHA reached GitHub; the 5-minute missing clock and the 60-minute
//                     unavailable clock start here, falling back to the commit's committer date.
//   --task <id>       a label copied into the wait record.
//
// Exit codes:
//   0   green: at least one run, every run concluded success, every required workflow present
//   1   red: a run concluded anything but success (failing jobs, steps, tests, and a log tail print)
//   2   missing: a required workflow, or any run at all, is absent 5 minutes after the push
//   3   unavailable: no readable `.github/ci-green.json` at the SHA, a git or API failure, no
//       terminal result 60 minutes after the push, or a usage error
//   75  pending: runs are still going (or the wait budget ran out); re-issue the same command
//
// Each `--wait` invocation appends one `kind: "ci"` line to the run records file shared with
// cairn-run-gate (`CAIRN_GATE_RECORDS_DIR`, default ~/.local/state/cairn-run-gate/runs.jsonl),
// under the same lock. The decision logic lives in ci-green-lib.mjs.
import { main } from "./ci-green-lib.mjs";

process.exitCode = main(process.argv.slice(2));
